WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:05.080
The WHO says there are no
proven effective therapeutics

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.220
to be had for the new coronavirus.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.040
Efforts are focused on
detection and containment.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:15.040
A vaccine is still at
least 12 to 18 months away.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.400 position:58%
As the coronavirus pandemic escalates
knowing we have such

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.840
a long wait until we can immunise
can be pretty scary.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:29.860
The coronavirus that causes
COVID-19 is a new virus and
attacks cells in a new way so

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.920
we need a new drug to tackle it.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.280
That said, there might already
be a drug ready to use,

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.560
we just don't know it yet.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.240 position:64%
Using pre-approved drugs is the best
strategy in the short term to help people

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:48.240
as others develop maybe more
sophisticated targeted approaches,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.840
say with a vaccine, or with
drugs that target the virus.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.480 position:64%
But that could take months, if not years.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.000
Professor Nevan Krogan works with
an international team of scientists

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.000
that have identified pre-existing
drugs with the potential
to fight the coronavirus.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:07.510
What we've noticed here is
that this virus, in a very
interesting way, is coming in and

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.200
essentially hijacking all the
major biological processes in the
cell, in a very fascinating way.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:18.970 position:66%
So it's getting its fingers in pretty
much all the major machinery in our cells.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:23.540
So the virus needs our cells
and our genes and proteins,
in order to live and replicate

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:24.540
and infect ourselves.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.480
So the question is,
what are those proteins?

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.600
So we generated this
map, this blueprint where

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.880
we're essentially, looking
for all the human proteins
connected to virus proteins.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.280
We identified 332 of these human
proteins, through this study.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.560
And predicted drugs, or compounds

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.520
that would come and bind to and
inhibit these human proteins.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:49.530
So of the 69 that we predicted
to bind at least one of these
332 proteins, 27 of which

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.000
are FDA-approved drugs and the
rest are in clinical trials, or
being looked at pre-clinically.

00:01:57.049 --> 00:02:01.070
The logic here is if we do get
a hit on one of these drugs
we can go and look at one of

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:05.700
these drugs in more detail and
then see what other drugs, or
compounds are similar to it.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.700
So we can expand our search
space, based on this initial
screen at the end of the day.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.920
Which will give us hopefully a
higher likelihood of success.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.630 position:64%
Give us a sense of why it's easier to use
an old drug?

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:23.630
I think on average it takes
eight, nine years to get a drug
into a person and a big reason

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.010
for that length of time is that you
have to go through toxicity trials.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:34.989
But if you focus on drugs, or
compounds, that have already
passed that barrier. You can

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.000
more quickly expedite a particular
drug, or compound, into a
person to target this virus.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.000
And a lot of times that's not
done, this drug repurposing.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.960
One of the reasons is,
financially related.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:53.040 position:66%
Companies have patents for specific
compounds, or drugs and if another company

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.989
finds that, oh, this drug could
be used for another disease.
Well the original company

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.800
will get all the financial
benefit from that.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.040
So people don't look at
those in that way that much.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.800 position:64%
But of course now everybody just wants to
solve this particular problem.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.840 position:61%
And I would say one of the classic
examples of drug repurposing is Viagra.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.120 position:64%
It was initially used to treat heart pain

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.600 position:64%
and then it was realized there were other
beneficial effects associated with it.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.629
So we'd been thinking
about this for some time.

00:03:20.629 --> 00:03:25.629
and obviously now, with
this sense of urgency, it's
really allowed us to expedite

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:28.080
this particular vision and test it.

